      {
        "id": "thm-marchenko-pastur-law-for-sample-covariance",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Almost-sure Marchenko–Pastur law for finite-fourth-moment sample covariance matrices",
        "deps": [
          "def-sample-covariance-empirical-spectral-distribution",
          "def-stieltjes-transform-of-a-real-probability-measure",
          "def-marchenko-pastur-measure",
          "lem-sample-covariance-empirical-law-is-a-probability-measure",
          "lem-fourth-moment-quadratic-form-resolvent-bound",
          "lem-rank-one-resolvent-update-and-trace-bound",
          "lem-adapted-resolvent-trace-concentration",
          "lem-stieltjes-transform-inversion-and-uniqueness-on-the-real-line",
          "def-weak-convergence-of-borel-probability-measures",
          "cor-tightness-extracts-a-weakly-convergent-subsequence",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice",
          "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "def-almost-sure-convergence-of-random-variables",
          "cor-first-borel-cantelli-lemma-for-events",
          "thm-chebyshev-inequality",
          "lem-marchenko-pastur-formula-defines-a-finite-measure"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 2,
      "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, and let all the matrices $X_n$ be defined on one common probability space. For each positive integer $n$, let $p_n$ be a positive integer with $p_n/n\\to\\gamma\\in(0,\\infty)$, and let $X_n\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p_n}$ have mutually independent entries. Assume that all entries of all the matrices $X_n$ have one common law with mean zero, variance one, and finite fourth moment. Set $S_n=X_n^{\\mathsf T}X_n/n$ and let $\\mu_n$ be its empirical spectral distribution normalized by $p_n$. Then $\\mu_n$ converges weakly almost surely to the probability measure with density\n$$\\rho_\\gamma(x)=\\begin{cases}\\dfrac{\\sqrt{(b_\\gamma-x)(x-a_\\gamma)}}{2\\pi\\gamma x},&a_\\gamma<x<b_\\gamma,\\\\0,&x\\notin(a_\\gamma,b_\\gamma),\\end{cases}\\qquad a_\\gamma=(1-\\sqrt\\gamma)^2,\\quad b_\\gamma=(1+\\sqrt\\gamma)^2,$$\nand an atom of mass $(1-1/\\gamma)_+$ at zero."
      },
